<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    DEVELOPER MEASUREMENT TOOL — NOT PART OF THE PRODUCT. See tools/probes/README.md.

    Domain-typed routine signatures (stabilization sprint S-1b, 2026-08-05). The user reported that
    changing a procedure PARAMETER's domain "does not save on compile", while the same change on a
    VARIABLE does. Reading the code found something worse than a failed save: the domain was discarded
    on READ, so the object editors — which reassemble the whole CREATE OR ALTER from what the read
    returned — wrote the resolved BASE type back. Opening a procedure whose parameters use domains and
    pressing Compile for any unrelated reason therefore destroyed the domain link in the database,
    silently. That is architecture rule #11 (never lose information), gotcha #175's shape.

    The unit tests pin the two decision points (TypeTextForField, EmitsNotNull) but they cannot prove
    the end-to-end claim, because the claim is about what the CATALOG holds and what a real
    reconstruction produces from it. This probe proves exactly that, on a live engine:

      (1) THE DOMAIN SURVIVES THE READ — the reconstructed CREATE OR ALTER for a procedure and a
          function names the domain (D_CODE / D_QTY / D_NAME), not its base type, while a plain
          parameter beside it still shows its base type.

      (2) THE ROUND TRIP IS LOSSLESS — recompiling the reconstruction and reading it again yields a
          BYTE-IDENTICAL definition. This is the assertion that actually covers the reported defect:
          it is precisely what the editors do on Compile, so if it holds, "open and compile" can no
          longer change the object. It would have FAILED before the fix.

      (3) NULLABILITY IS NOT INVENTED — a parameter on a NOT NULL domain must not come back carrying an
          explicit NOT NULL the original declaration never had, and a parameter that DID declare
          NOT NULL must keep it. Measured on FB5 first: the parameter's own RDB$NULL_FLAG and the
          domain's are two different facts, and which one applies depends on which type is emitted.

      (4) THE DEBUGGER IS UNAFFECTED — FirebirdDebugMetadata resolves a domain-typed parameter to its
          BASE type on purpose (spec R2: a domain-constrained parameter cannot take an injected NULL),
          and it shares IsUserDomain with the DDL reader. The two needs are opposite, so the probe
          checks that the debugger's frame layout still reports base types.

    Creates and drops its OWN scratch database at an ASCII path (gotcha #149) — the lab is never
    touched, so the probe is safe to run at any time. Deliberately NOT in EmberTern.slnx: it needs a
    live server and a password.

    How to run: see the header of Program.cs.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\EmberTern.Core\EmberTern.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\EmberTern.Firebird\EmberTern.Firebird.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
